A||||5||sqoach||thermostat doesn't snap in place||||11||
Z||000000||sqoach||12-02-2000||08:57 PM||||is the thermostat suppose to snap in place?  i pushed on it pretty hard and realized that it wasn't going to snap in.  any thoughts on this?  my thermostat housing is kinda corroded some so that might be it.<P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh<P>fiero7@hotmail.com||||reg||11
Z||000001||fropuff||12-02-2000||09:24 PM||||I had the same problem with it not going all the way down.  I ended up bending two thermostats when putting the cap back on.  I pushed too hard, and the arm on the thermostat bent, causing a bind so it wouldn't open.  Someone told me to use a long screwdriver to push it in all the way.  Work your way around the base of the thersostat.  If the cap still doesn't go on, try it again.  I don't know what to tell you about the corrosion.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||12-03-2000||04:18 AM||||I just kinda hammer it down in there.  Never had a problem with getting it out again...||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||12-03-2000||06:16 AM||||Try a little Vasoline in the O ring on the thermostat.||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||12-03-2000||01:57 PM||||Ease it in, using a long screwdriver to press down on the base of the thermostat.  Otherwise, if you aren't careful, you'll buckle the "bail".  The "bail" on the 'stat should be even with the top of the water outlet before you install the cap.<P>I happen to <B>like</B> the fact that you can change the thermostat without scraping off old/ purchasing new gaskets     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">!<P>--------------------------------------<BR>Oh-! To answer your original question more directly, I think that o-ring seals set up like the one on the Fiero thermostat, aren't ordinarily supposed to "snap" into place.  They install into a constant-diameter bore (with a tapered lead-in), so that the o-ring gets a fixed amount of radial compression. Then you need something to hold it in, to react the pressure.  That's (part of) what the bail+cap do.<P>O-ring setups like this are used for the 2.8 distributor-to-block seal, and on Corvair pushrod tubes.  On the 2.8, the distributor hold-down clamp holds things in place; on the Corvair, pushrod guide plates do the job.  Nothing "snaps" in place - and tightening the hold-down feature won't cure a leak - you have to take it apart and replace the o-ring.         <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 12-03-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000005||theogre||12-03-2000||02:54 PM||||The section of the housing where the thermostat o ring seals has a tapered leadin. What many people describe as a feeling like it is snapping in is most likely the transition from the taper to the actual sealing surface.<P>I usually coat mine with a little vasaline, dielectric, or if nothing else a film of tranny fluid. All you want is a film on the ring so it slides into place. There's not enough to contaminate the coolant.<P>Unless the neck is rather corroded or dented you should not need much force to push it down. Especially with the ring lubricated.||||reg||1
